well we're going to get more information  very soon on how lethal this is and  whether it's susceptible to the vaccines  we now have but i think we've been right  to say everybody should be getting  vaccinated where possible everybody  should be tested where there's a risk  and that's true of our country but it  must be true of the rest of the world as  well and particularly africa so few  people have been vaccinated only seven  percent  so few have been tested hardly any tests  are being done in africa so you don't  detect things quickly enough so when the  g7 ministers meet this morning they  should agree that they will coordinate a  month-by-month plan to get vaccines out  to africa and to help them get testing  equipment and when the world health  assembly meets which is this morning  they should agree on a new uh treaty so  that we can prevent pandemics and deal  with them properly in the years to come  i did speak to the government minister  this morning the health minister in fact  and he was saying that you know we have  sent as part of the kovacs scheme we  have sent vaccines to africa and other  parts of the world and we will continue  to do that and we'll be up to about a  third of the level that we've committed  to very soon  i don't think that's strictly correct i  mean boris johnson said on  saturday that britain was leading the  way  we offered 100 million vaccines we've  provided only 11 of them so far  america has offered a billion vaccines  and provided 25 percent and even that's  not enough so britain's records not very  good and when boris johnson said on  saturday that the real problem in africa  was that people were not taking up the  vaccines  he was wrong because the real problem in  africa the biggest problem is the lack  of supply and as soon as we could get  the supply out to africa then i'm pretty  sure that the countries that have been  identified as red spots by the british  government  we can get vaccines into people's arms  it doesn't help us when there has been a  collective failure to get vaccines out  to everybody in the world you know  low-income countries it's three percent  britain it's nearly 70 percent what a  difference despite the fact that  everybody should have access to this  vaccine  and dr congo is 0.6 uh fully vaccinated  i think i was reading this morning  former prime minister world health  assembly uh getting underway today it's  a three-day  session examining global preparedness  and responses you know what is it that  you want to come out of that  well there should have been far better  early warning systems  there should also have been a far better  exchange of medical goods when people  were in difficulty and of course there  should have been equity in the  distribution of vaccines all these  should have happened and i think what  the world health assembly should do is  say look we want what you might call a  pandemic non-proliferation treaty we  want to prevent this happening in future  we've got to act now of course and get  the vaccines out but we've got to be  prepared for further variance and for  further  outrage outbreaks and we don't want them  to become pandemic so let's have a new  treaty where each country binds itself  to take the early action that should  have been taken and should still be  taken when it comes to vaccines  so what should we be doing to try to  protect the british public though as  well do we literally need to pull up the  drawbridge every time we hear about a  new variant  look um i i've been very clear speaking  on behalf of the world health  organization in this instance  that we've got to get vaccines out to  the rest of the world and there are  probably about 100 million vaccines in  the west that will be wasted by the end  of the year because they're not going to  be used and i think people hate waste  and hate the idea that vaccines will  pass their expiry date and not be used  as far as britain is concerned i had you  interviewing angela rainer and the  health minister already today and what  they're saying is absolutely right  you've got to act quickly  in september i think the sage committee  recommended the wider use of masks that  has got to come in obviously  and people have got to take the advice  about hand washing the social distancing  and so on if we're going to avoid some  of the problems that came last christmas  this christmas and of course if we're  going to avoid this going into the new  year we've got to take this early action  it's pretty clear that that's what's  necessary  so a couple of things to unpack their  former prime minister first of all as  far as masks are concerned the  government says you don't need to wear a  mask in england if you are in a pub or  in a restaurant obviously if you're  eating drinking of course you're not  going to but what about if you're  standing at the bar if you're in  scotland of course you know that you do  do you think that that's something the  government should be looking at again  well i think that advice is probably  going to change in the next few few days  i've always been very careful not to  second-guess the medical advice but i  think the medical advice from september  has been pretty clear about the wider  use of masks  and of course we now have some cases in  scotland this this morning as well as  cases in england uh from the weekend and  i think people will be far more vigilant  in future and will be wearing massive  from tomorrow of course uh in england uh  there's new laws that will require  people to do so in public transport uh  and if they're in shops and as far as  pcr tests are concerned the government  again decided that they weren't  necessary uh when people were either two  days before you came back to the uk or  indeed the two days after you were back  in the uk didn't need them uh anymore do  you think those should be reintroduced  as a matter of course i'm talking  particularly about the ones in order to  get into the uk  yes i think this this issue about taking  tests before you come to the uk will  come to the fore again when the g7  health ministers meet this morning there  probably should be a collective  agreement uh that will all do roughly  the same things when we're worried about  this you know that japan has already  closed flights you know that israel's  already done it morocco has already done  it  so you're going to have different  countries acting in different ways and a  lot of confusion it might be better if  the g7 could agree a collective approach  to what you do about testing before you  come to a particular country and of  course testing once you come in into a  country but you know the general point i  want to make is that  if we're talking about things that must  be done in the united kingdom and i  agree get vaccinated if you haven't been  vaccinated get testing if you haven't  been test tested and you think there is  a problem  then that advice must apply to the whole  of the world otherwise this disease will  continue to spread somewhere else it  will continue to mutate and it will come  back to haunt us even the fully  vaccinated if we haven't taken action in  other countries where the disease is  spreading some of those countries  particularly southern african countries  are saying by the time we get these  vaccines that you've promised us the the  shelf life is so short on them they're  useless and we can't have a a campaign  uh concerted advertising campaign to  make sure people do get vaccinated there  is a consideration a thought that  actually the um use by date on these  vaccines is just too short is that  something that should be looked at again  yeah that that is a real problem because  we talk about leftovers when you've got  extra vaccines you get them out but  probably at the last minute uh to the  countries that are needing them the  better way is to think look we're  producing soon two billion vaccines a  month across the world there's going to  be no difficulty about countries being  able to get hold of them if they're rich  enough to be able to afford them over  the next few months if we have excess  supply and we know we're not going to  use them in the next few months then we  should get them out as quickly as  possible this is all about coordination  uh if the g20 the group of richest  countries could agree that they would  have a coordinator so that we had a  month by month plan then we would not  run the same risk of a hundred million  vaccines being simply wasted now if you  waste a vaccine you're actually putting  a life at risk because that vaccine  could have been a life-saving uh it's  something that could have saved lives in  in another country if not our own  yeah and and just as i'm talking to you  hearing from the scottish deputy first  minister saying that there's no travel  history in some of the cases of the  omicron variant that have just been  discovered in scotland that would  suggest  perhaps it didn't originate in south  africa  well we know now that the first um  news of uh of a potential problem was  november the 11th  and i think it was in botswana and and  we will find more in the next few days  about where this originated what its  lethality is as well as how  transmissible it is so we need that  medical expertise luckily we've got one  of the best sequencing operations in the  united kingdom to enable us to do this  and south africa is cooperating in a way  that has been uh helpful to getting to  the bottom of this so i i do believe  we'll find out more information in the  next few days and of course the key  question is is the vaccine that is in  our arms or coming into our arms are  going to be able to cope with this new  variant and i certainly hope and i think  everybody will hope that it can

